    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d572">

                <pb facs="0621" n="541" sameAs="frus:frus1930v01/pg541"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.7422/51</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Canadian Secretary of State for External
                        Affairs</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Mackenzie
                            King</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the American Chargé in
                        Canada</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Riggs</hi>)<anchor n="42" xml:id="d572-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Ottawa</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1930-06-19">19 June, 1930</date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 71</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: With reference to my note No. 70 of even
                        date<anchor n="43" xml:id="d572-f2"/> and to previous <pb facs="0622"
                        n="542" xml:id="pg542"/> correspondence regarding the Aviation Radio
                    Conference held in New York on April 10 and 11 last, I have the honour to inform
                    you that the Canadian Government are prepared to accept the recommendations of
                    the Conference and, in developing its radio “aids to air navigation and radio
                    communication facilities with aircraft”, will follow the general principle set
                    out in these recommendations.</p>
                <p>Accept [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">O. D. Skelton</hi></signed><lb/>For the
                    Secretary of State for External Affairs</closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d572-f1" n="42" type="source" xml:id="d572-f1-content">
                <p>Copy transmitted to the Department by the Chargé as an enclosure to his despatch
                    No. 1466, June 23, 1930; received June 30.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d572-f2" n="43" xml:id="d572-f2-content">
